<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="p1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\svgpath</span>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p">figs/</p>
</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document" style="font-size:173%;">Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Vadivelan Murugesan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, Tandon School of Engineering, New York University, 6 MetroTech Center, Brooklyn, NY 11201, USA.
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Rajasundaram Mathiazhagan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, Tandon School of Engineering, New York University, 6 MetroTech Center, Brooklyn, NY 11201, USA.
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sanjana Joshi
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, Tandon School of Engineering, New York University, 6 MetroTech Center, Brooklyn, NY 11201, USA.
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aliasghar Arab
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Human–robot collaboration requires precise prediction of human motion over extended horizons to enable proactive collision avoidance. Unlike existing planners that rely solely on kinodynamic models, we present a prediction-driven safe planning framework that leverages granular, joint-by-joint human motion forecasting validated in a physics-based digital twin. A capsule-based artificial potential field (APF) converts these granular predictions into collision risk metrics, triggering an Adaptive RRT* (A-RRT*) planner when thresholds are exceeded. The depth camera is used to extract 3D skeletal poses and a convolutional neural network–bidirectional long short-term memory (CNN–BiLSTM) model to predict individual joint trajectories ahead of time. A digital twin model integrates real-time human posture prediction placed in front of a simulated robot to evaluate motions and physical contacts. The proposed method enables validation of planned trajectories ahead of time and bridging potential latency gaps in updating planned trajectories in real-time. In 50 trials, our method achieved 100% proactive avoidance with <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;\,$250\text{\,}\mathrm{mm}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.448em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>250</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">mm</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;\,$250\text{\,}\mathrm{mm}$</annotation></semantics></math> clearance and sub-2 s replanning, demonstrating superior precision and reliability compared to existing kinematic-only planners through the integration of predictive human modeling with digital twin validation.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">INTRODUCTION</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human–robot collaboration (HRC) shares workspace between people and manipulators, demanding safety without sacrificing throughput. Reactive safeguards that respond after threshold breaches are often too slow or disruptive <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. This work adopts proactive collision avoidance: the robot follows its nominal task while continuously predicting human motion and intervening only when a predicted clearance violation is likely <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Time‑varying constraints (human clearance, joint limits, self‑collision) preserve productivity while maintaining minimum separation. If predicted risk exceeds a threshold, an APF‑guided A‑RRT* replans a collision‑free path.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/digitaltwin.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:334/236;" width="334" height="236" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Digital-twin interface used for human-aware planning.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/real2sim2real.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/138;" width="476" height="138" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">End-to-end Digital-Twin planning pipeline for a Real to Simulaiton to Real.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Model predictions enable planning ahead of time by forecasting human motion before conflicts occur. Early methods used recurrent models for short-horizon joint predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Recent work includes intention cues and hand prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, semi-Markov modeling for assembly behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, and context-aware manufacturing predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Deep learning dominates with Transformer intent models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, lightweight HRI predictors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and learning-from-demonstration for pose prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Uncertainty is addressed with deep ensembles and hybrid physics–ML designs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. The CNN–BiLSTM predictor in this study enables prediction of a joint trajectory one second ahead from a three-second observation window.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The safety fields and the risk metrics define the objectives for optimization of the motion planning. An APF provides fast and interpretable proximity costs for pHRI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Vision-driven safety fuses perception with APF or distance fields <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. For manipulators, APF shapes have been refined with improved attractive/reactive terms and velocity feedforward <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. APF–RRT* hybrids mitigate local minima and bias exploration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. A capsule-based APF is adopted that scores present and predicted proximities with horizon weighting. This converts anticipated proximities into a scalar risk that gates planning intervention, enabling optimization for both task completion and safety.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Digital twins enable accurate modeling using real-world data in high-accuracy physics engines, following a real-to-sim-to-real paradigm. They offer safe high-fidelity testing for HRC before deployment, including painting, VR disassembly, retail training, and assembly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Reviews analyze pipelines, human models, and sustainability in Industry 4.0/5.0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. Implementations include EKF-based monitoring on UR platforms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Live pose and planner state are mirrored in Gazebo/RViz for repeatable testing and threshold tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. Physics engine studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> motivate validation in a ROS 2 twin that exposes the same topics and controllers as hardware.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This study introduces a prediction‑driven safety framework for HRC that (i) estimates 3D human joints from RGB–D/IMU and predicts joint‑by‑joint motion one second ahead with a CNN–BiLSTM, (ii) converts present and predicted proximities into a scalar risk via a capsule‑based artificial potential field (APF) that triggers a GPU‑accelerated A‑RRT* when a threshold <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is exceeded, and (iii) validates and visualizes the closed loop in a ROS 2 digital twin (Gazebo/RViz). In 50 trials, the system achieved 100% proactive avoidance with <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;\,$250\text{\,}\mathrm{mm}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.448em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>250</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">mm</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;\,$250\text{\,}\mathrm{mm}$</annotation></semantics></math> clearance and 0.1–2.0 s replans.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">RRT* variants need costs that reflect human motion, with recent work coupling learned predictors with A-RRT* and embedding APF in sampling/smoothing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. The latency gap is addressed by vectorizing FK, APF evaluation, and interpolation on the GPU. For example, in a pick operation with a UR16e, while the human remains stationary, the manipulator follows the nominal shortest path. If the human moves and predicted arm position enters the robot’s future path, APF rises above <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, the controller pauses, and A-RRT* returns an alternate path maintaining required clearance. Once risk subsides, the nominal path resumes, preserving operator expectations while ensuring safety.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/ntwrk.png" id="S1.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/232;" width="476" height="232" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S1.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CNN–BiLSTM human-motion predictor. Input: 3 s (10 Hz) sequences of bone unit vectors and joint displacements for 15 joints. A 1D convolution stage captures short-term kinematics; stacked bidirectional LSTMs model long-horizon temporal dependencies. A fully connected decoder outputs 10-frame (1 s) displacement and bone-orientation predictions, reconstructed into full 3D poses.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PROBLEM FORMULATION</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With configuration <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathcal{C}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathcal{C}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and control input <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathcal{U}\subset\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathcal{U}\subset\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The system evolves according to control-affine dynamics.</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{q}=f(q,u)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{q}=f(q,u),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz.
The environment <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> includes static obstacles, a human, and a task object.
Observations are denoted as <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o=o(q,\mathcal{E}_{t})\in\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o=o(q,\mathcal{E}_{t})\in\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume a prediction horizon of <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> steps with sampling time <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> and times <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}=k\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}=k\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,\dots,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,\dots,N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human pose.</span> The predicted human pose at <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random variable <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with mean <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{H}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{H}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and covariance <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{H}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{H}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The uncertainty tube is</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k}=\left\{h:\|h-\mu^{H}_{k}\|_{(\Sigma^{H}_{k})^{-1}}\leq\rho^{H}_{k}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k}=\left\{h:\|h-\mu^{H}_{k}\|_{(\Sigma^{H}_{k})^{-1}}\leq\rho^{H}_{k}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safety Sets and Distances</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\min}(q,h,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\min}(q,h,s)</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimum signed distance between the robot (at configuration <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>) and any obstacle given human pose <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and object pose <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle X_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle X_{u}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\{(q,o):d_{\min}(q,h,s)&lt;0\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\{(q,o):d_{\min}(q,h,s)&lt;0\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\{(q,o):d_{\min}(q,h,s)\geq r_{\text{thres}}\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>thres</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\{(q,o):d_{\min}(q,h,s)\geq r_{\text{thres}}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle X_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle X_{b}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=X\setminus(X_{u}\cup X_{s})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=X\setminus(X_{u}\cup X_{s}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{thres}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>thres</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{thres}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> ensures a safety margin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Uncertainty is incorporated either by robust inflation:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,o)\in X_{s}\;\;\;\forall(h,s)\in\mathcal{H}_{k}\times\mathcal{S}_{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,o)\in X_{s}\;\;\;\forall(h,s)\in\mathcal{H}_{k}\times\mathcal{S}_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">or by chance constraints:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\!\left(d_{\min}(q,H_{k},S_{k})\geq r_{\text{safe}}\right)\geq 1-\delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.441em;" width="0.441em"><mi>ℙ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\!\left(d_{\min}(q,H_{k},S_{k})\geq r_{\text{safe}}\right)\geq 1-\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}\notin X_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}\notin X_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and a goal set <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\text{goal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mtext>goal</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\text{goal}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the planner seeks a control sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{0:N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{0:N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizing</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\Big[\ell(q_{k},u_{k})+\lambda_{H}\phi_{H}(q_{k};\mathcal{H}_{k})+\lambda_{S}\phi_{S}(q_{k};\mathcal{S}_{k})\Big]+\psi(q_{N})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\Big[\ell(q_{k},u_{k})+\lambda_{H}\phi_{H}(q_{k};\mathcal{H}_{k})+\lambda_{S}\phi_{S}(q_{k};\mathcal{S}_{k})\Big]+\psi(q_{N}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">subject to</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q_{k}+\int_{t_{k}}^{t_{k+1}}(f(q)+g(q)u_{k})\,dt," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q_{k}+\int_{t_{k}}^{t_{k+1}}(f(q)+g(q)u_{k})\,dt,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(q_{k},o_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(q_{k},o_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\notin X_{u}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\notin X_{u},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\mathcal{C}_{\text{goal}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mtext>goal</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\mathcal{C}_{\text{goal}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is a stage cost, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{H},\phi_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{H},\phi_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> are soft penalties for proximity to predicted human/object poses, and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> a terminal cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PREDICTIVE PLANNING IN DIGITAL TWIN</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the prediction-driven safe planning framework that leverages granular, joint-by-joint human motion forecasting validated in a physics-based digital twin. The framework consists of three integrated components: (i) RGB–D/IMU perception with CNN–BiLSTM joint trajectory prediction, (ii) capsule-based APF risk scoring that triggers GPU-accelerated A-RRT* replanning, and (iii) ROS 2 digital twin validation enabling real-time trajectory evaluation and latency gap bridging. The full pipeline is summarized in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Perception and 3D Joint Extraction</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RGB–D and IMU data from an Orbbec Femto Bolt are processed with MediaPipe Pose to obtain 2D landmarks, which are lifted to world-frame 3D using camera intrinsics and aligned depth. Given a pixel location <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding 3D point <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y,Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y,Z)</annotation></semantics></math> in the camera frame is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}X\\
Y\\
Z\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{(u-c_{x})\cdot D(u,v)}{f_{x}}\\
D(u,v)\\
\frac{(c_{y}-v)\cdot D(u,v)}{f_{y}}\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>Y</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>Z</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}X\\
Y\\
Z\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{(u-c_{x})\cdot D(u,v)}{f_{x}}\\
D(u,v)\\
\frac{(c_{y}-v)\cdot D(u,v)}{f_{y}}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> are the focal lengths and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> are the principal point coordinates of the camera.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frame convention.</span> We adopt a camera frame with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+Y</annotation></semantics></math> pointing forward (depth), <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+X</annotation></semantics></math> to the right, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+Z</annotation></semantics></math> upward; image coordinates use <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to the right and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> downward. Under this convention <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=D(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=D(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> follows <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{y}-v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{y}-v)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In III-A Perception and 3D Joint Extraction ‣ III PREDICTIVE PLANNING IN DIGITAL TWIN ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). The IMU-based rotation <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{y}(\beta)R_{x}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{y}(\beta)R_{x}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> then aligns this camera frame to the world frame.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To account for sensor tilt, accelerometer data from the Orbbec IMU is used to compute roll (<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) and pitch (<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) angles. The rotation matrices about the <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> axes are:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{x}(\alpha)=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;\cos\alpha&amp;-\sin\alpha\\
0&amp;\sin\alpha&amp;\cos\alpha\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{x}(\alpha)=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;\cos\alpha&amp;-\sin\alpha\\
0&amp;\sin\alpha&amp;\cos\alpha\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{y}(\beta)=\begin{bmatrix}\cos\beta&amp;0&amp;\sin\beta\\
0&amp;1&amp;0\\
-\sin\beta&amp;0&amp;\cos\beta\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{y}(\beta)=\begin{bmatrix}\cos\beta&amp;0&amp;\sin\beta\\
0&amp;1&amp;0\\
-\sin\beta&amp;0&amp;\cos\beta\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The final transformation to world coordinates is:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{world}}=R_{y}(\beta)\,R_{x}(\alpha)\,\mathbf{P}_{\text{cam}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>world</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>cam</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{world}}=R_{y}(\beta)\,R_{x}(\alpha)\,\mathbf{P}_{\text{cam}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{cam}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>cam</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{cam}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the 3D point in the camera frame. We denote <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{wc}(\alpha,\beta)=R_{y}(\beta)R_{x}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{wc}(\alpha,\beta)=R_{y}(\beta)R_{x}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> for later reference; i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{world}}=T_{wc}\,\mathbf{P}_{\text{cam}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>world</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>cam</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{world}}=T_{wc}\,\mathbf{P}_{\text{cam}}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A Perception and 3D Joint Extraction ‣ III PREDICTIVE PLANNING IN DIGITAL TWIN ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following 15 biomechanical joints are extracted and tracked:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Upper body:</span> CLAV (Clavicle midpoint), C7 (7th Cervical Vertebra), LSHO (Left Shoulder), RSHO (Right Shoulder), LAEL (Left Elbow), RAEL (Right Elbow), LWPS (Left Wrist), RWPS (Right Wrist)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spine and Pelvis:</span> L3 (Lumbar vertebra 3, interpolated between C7 and pelvis), LHIP (Left Hip), RHIP (Right Hip)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lower body:</span> LKNE (Left Knee), RKNE (Right Knee), LHEE (Left Heel), RHEE (Right Heel)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">From RGB-D to Planning Coordinates.</span> The extracted 3D joint positions <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{world}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>world</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{world}}</annotation></semantics></math> from equations (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In III-A Perception and 3D Joint Extraction ‣ III PREDICTIVE PLANNING IN DIGITAL TWIN ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A Perception and 3D Joint Extraction ‣ III PREDICTIVE PLANNING IN DIGITAL TWIN ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-A Perception and 3D Joint Extraction ‣ III PREDICTIVE PLANNING IN DIGITAL TWIN ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), and (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A Perception and 3D Joint Extraction ‣ III PREDICTIVE PLANNING IN DIGITAL TWIN ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) directly provide the human pose <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> used in the planning problem. For each joint <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the world-frame position <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{j}=\mathbf{P}_{\text{world}}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>world</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{j}=\mathbf{P}_{\text{world}}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the human configuration that appears in the safety constraints of equations (3)–(5), where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\min}(q,h,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\min}(q,h,s)</annotation></semantics></math> evaluates clearance between the robot configuration <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and the observed human pose <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Human Motion Prediction</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human motion forecasting in collaborative robotics must balance <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temporal accuracy</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">low latency</span>, and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">robustness to sensor noise</span>. We predict the subject’s 3D skeletal motion up to one second into the future (10 frames at 10 Hz) from a three-second observation window (30 frames), leveraging skeleton-based deep sequence modeling to capture spatial structure and temporal dynamics for proactive replanning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>1 </span>Input Representation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RGB, depth, and IMU streams from the Orbbec Femto Bolt are processed by <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MediaPipe Pose</span> to obtain 33 landmarks per frame; these are lifted to <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3D world coordinates</span> using intrinsics, aligned depth, and IMU tilt compensation (Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). From these, we retain 15 biomechanical joints relevant to collaboration:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{J}=\{" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{J}=\{</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{CLAV},\text{C7},\text{LSHO},\text{RSHO},\text{LAEL},\text{RAEL}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>CLAV</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>C7</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>LSHO</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>RSHO</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>LAEL</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>RAEL</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{CLAV},\text{C7},\text{LSHO},\text{RSHO},\text{LAEL},\text{RAEL},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex1Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{LWPS},\text{RWPS},\text{L3},\text{LHIP},\text{RHIP}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>LWPS</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>RWPS</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>L3</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>LHIP</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>RHIP</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{LWPS},\text{RWPS},\text{L3},\text{LHIP},\text{RHIP},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex1Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{LKNE},\text{RKNE},\text{LHEE},\text{RHEE}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>LKNE</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>RKNE</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>LHEE</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>RHEE</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{LKNE},\text{RKNE},\text{LHEE},\text{RHEE}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each frame is normalized into <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bone unit vectors</em> (directions between connected joints) and <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">joint displacement vectors</em> (frame-to-frame changes), which reduces dependence on global position and improves invariance to viewpoint and camera placement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>2 </span>Network Architecture</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CNN–BiLSTM hybrid</span> (Fig. <a href="#S1.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). A 1D convolution layer captures short-horizon kinematic patterns in the bone/displacement sequences; stacked bidirectional LSTMs encode longer-term temporal dependencies; and a fully connected decoder outputs predicted joint displacements and bone orientations for the next 10 frames (1 s ahead). The predicted poses <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are reconstructed by binding the bone vectors to the root joint (L3) to recover the full 3D skeletal coordinates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>3 </span>Loss Function</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training minimizes a weighted sum of position error and orientation mismatch.</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{p}\,\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{P}_{t},P_{t})\;+\;\lambda_{b}\left(1-\frac{\hat{b}_{t}\cdot b_{t}}{\|\hat{b}_{t}\|\,\|b_{t}\|}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>MSE</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{p}\,\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{P}_{t},P_{t})\;+\;\lambda_{b}\left(1-\frac{\hat{b}_{t}\cdot b_{t}}{\|\hat{b}_{t}\|\,\|b_{t}\|}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This balances accurate joint placement with consistent limb orientations across the horizon.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B3 Loss Function ‣ III-B Human Motion Prediction ‣ III PREDICTIVE PLANNING IN DIGITAL TWIN ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the model outputs a one-second
prediction horizon (red) overlayed on the measured test split pose (blue).These predictions are published at 10 Hz and evaluated by the capsule-based APF to trigger A-RRT* when future waypoints are unsafe.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/mtprd.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/464;" width="429" height="464" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Test-split visualization after training. Blue points/links show the current measured 3D pose from a held-out test sequence; red shows the network’s 10-step (1 s) forecast in world coordinates. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Risk Metric: Capsule APF</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Robot links and human limbs are modeled as capsules. For robot surface samples <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and predicted human link capsules <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> at the next step <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\sum_{i}\sum_{j}\sum_{k}w_{i}\,\Phi(d_{ijk})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\sum_{i}\sum_{j}\sum_{k}w_{i}\,\Phi(d_{ijk}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ijk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ijk}</annotation></semantics></math> is the shortest distance to the capsule axis and</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(d)=\begin{cases}2,&amp;d&lt;0\\
\cos\!\left(\frac{\pi d}{2d_{\text{th}}}\right),&amp;0\leq d\leq d_{\text{th}}\\
0,&amp;d&gt;d_{\text{th}}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.216em;" width="1.216em"><mi>cos</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(d)=\begin{cases}2,&amp;d&lt;0\\
\cos\!\left(\frac{\pi d}{2d_{\text{th}}}\right),&amp;0\leq d\leq d_{\text{th}}\\
0,&amp;d&gt;d_{\text{th}}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Weights <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> downweight farther-horizon predictions. If <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, the planner is invoked.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A-RRT* with APF-Guided Sampling</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Planning occurs in <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> joint space. We bias sampling toward the goal (Gaussian goal bias), prune samples that violate the APF threshold, and use a bidirectional connection strategy. Steering maintains angular continuity via modulo <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{\text{new}}=\mathbf{q}_{1}+\Delta\cdot\frac{(\mathbf{q}_{2}-\mathbf{q}_{1}+\pi)\bmod 2\pi-\pi}{\|\cdot\|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{\text{new}}=\mathbf{q}_{1}+\Delta\cdot\frac{(\mathbf{q}_{2}-\mathbf{q}_{1}+\pi)\bmod 2\pi-\pi}{\|\cdot\|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">GPU vectorization accelerates forward kinematics, APF evaluation, and interpolation. During execution, future waypoints of the active trajectory are continuously rescored; violations pause motion and trigger replanning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. The planner pseudocode is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-D A-RRT* with APF-Guided Sampling ‣ III PREDICTIVE PLANNING IN DIGITAL TWIN ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> APF-guided A-RRT* for proactive HRC</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
            start <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, goal <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, threshold <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, time cap <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
            <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{s}\leftarrow\{q_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{s}\leftarrow\{q_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{g}\leftarrow\{q_{g}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{g}\leftarrow\{q_{g}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">and</span> not connected <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{rand}}\leftarrow\textsc{GaussianSampleAround}(q_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>rand</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">GaussianSampleAround</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{rand}}\leftarrow\textsc{GaussianSampleAround}(q_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> with 10% goal bias

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{APF}(q_{\text{rand}})\geq\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>APF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>rand</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{APF}(q_{\text{rand}})\geq\tau</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
               <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">continue</span>   
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{near}}\leftarrow\textsc{Nearest}(\mathcal{T}_{s},q_{\text{rand}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>near</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Nearest</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>rand</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{near}}\leftarrow\textsc{Nearest}(\mathcal{T}_{s},q_{\text{rand}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{new}}\leftarrow\textsc{Steer}(q_{\text{near}},q_{\text{rand}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Steer</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>near</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>rand</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{new}}\leftarrow\textsc{Steer}(q_{\text{near}},q_{\text{rand}})</annotation></semantics></math> with modulo-<math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi</annotation></semantics></math> continuity

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{APF}(q_{\text{new}})&lt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>APF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{APF}(q_{\text{new}})&lt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">and</span> <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CollisionFree</span> <span id="alg1.l9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
               add <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{new}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>; optionally <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Rewire</span> neighbors
  
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConnectBidirectional</span>(<math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{s},\mathcal{T}_{g},q_{\text{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>new</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{s},\mathcal{T}_{g},q_{\text{new}}</annotation></semantics></math>) <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
               <span id="alg1.l12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">break</span>   
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              swap <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T}_{s},\mathcal{T}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T}_{s},\mathcal{T}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>; update <math id="alg1.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> smoothed path if connected; else <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fail</span>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EXPERIMENTS</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implementation Details</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Software/Hardware.</span> The system is implemented in ROS 2 with Gazebo/RViz visualization. Human pose (current and predicted) is rendered as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MarkerArray</span> spheres (joints) and cylinders (bones) as shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The robot controller is the standard <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">joint_trajectory_controller</span>; trajectories are sent via <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">JointTrajectory</span> messages. Joint feedback from <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/joint_states</span> ensures smooth tracking; angles are wrapped to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-\pi,\pi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-\pi,\pi]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallelism.</span> A multi-threaded executor separates perception/prediction, risk scoring, planning, and control, keeping the loop responsive at 10 Hz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation is performed in a ROS 2 digital twin (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>): a UR16e performs pick-and-place while human pose (measured and predicted) streams in real-time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. Scenarios:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S1:</span> Static human near the base.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S2:</span> Human walking past the robot.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S3:</span> Partial occlusion of joints.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each scenario is repeated 20 times (60 trials in total). Planning latency, minimum clearance, and replanning frequency are recorded; summary metrics appear in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-C Visualization ‣ V RESULTS ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>. Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-C Visualization ‣ V RESULTS ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> summarizes key performance indicators across all trials: the A-RRT* planner explored 200 nodes on average, with 73 nodes used in final paths, achieving 275 mm minimum clearance from the closest human joint. The system achieved 100% proactive replanning coverage when APF threshold <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=20</annotation></semantics></math> was exceeded, maintaining 10 Hz real-time control with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;200</annotation></semantics></math> ms end-to-end latency from prediction to actuation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RESULTS</span></h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Planning Performance</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-C Visualization ‣ V RESULTS ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> compares planning times across existing methods. A CPU-only baseline yields 6–60 s planning times depending on proximity and predicted motion. Improved APF methods achieve 0.356–0.391 s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. With GPU offloading and algorithmic improvements (goal biasing, Gaussian sampling, bidirectional connection, continuity handling, APF pruning), our method achieves 0.1–2.0 s planning times, representing a 30–600<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup over the baseline and 3–18<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> improvement over existing APF methods, supporting continuous and interruption-free manipulation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/p1.png" id="S5.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/352;" width="476" height="352" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.sf1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Start</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/p2.png" id="S5.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.sf2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Intermediate</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/p3.png" id="S5.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/365;" width="476" height="365" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.sf3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S5.F5.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Goal</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Baseline execution without predicted interference. (a) UR16e initiates the cycle. (b) Nominal shortest path while APF for all forecasted waypoints remains <math id="S5.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. (c) Task completes without replanning. The yellow curve shows the commanded trajectory.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/c1.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/351;" width="476" height="351" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/c2.png" id="S5.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/353;" width="476" height="353" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/c3.png" id="S5.F6.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/386;" width="476" height="386" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03496v1/Figs/c4.png" id="S5.F6.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/373;" width="476" height="373" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 6</span>: </span><span id="S5.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Proactive replanning under predicted human motion (left<math id="S5.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>right). (1) Forecaster predicts intrusion into the shared workspace. (2) APF for future waypoints exceeds <math id="S5.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. (3) Execution pauses; GPU A-RRT* samples and prunes APF-unsafe nodes, biasing toward the goal. (4) A safe path is committed and executed, maintaining clearance while the task proceeds.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Collision Avoidance</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the APF metric with threshold <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\!=\!20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\!=\!20</annotation></semantics></math>, the system triggers
<em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proactive</em> replanning whenever any forecasted human–robot
configuration within the 1 s horizon yields a risk score above <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
Risk is evaluated both at the current pose and along the time-parameterized
prefix of the active trajectory, so the planner is invoked <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">before</em>
the geometric clearance falls near the influence radius <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Upon a trigger, the controller briefly holds the arm, the GPU A-RRT*
search expands while pruning APF-unsafe nodes, and a smoothed,
time-scaled alternative is committed; execution then resumes from the
nearest safe waypoint.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across 50 trials, triggers occurred in all cases where the forecaster
predicted workspace intrusion, with no missed events (100% coverage).
Minimum human–robot clearance remained greater than
 <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1.m1" class="ltx_markedasmath" alttext="250\text{\,}\mathrm{mm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>250</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">mm</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">250\text{\,}\mathrm{mm}</annotation></semantics></math> throughout; the worst observed clearance was
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2.m1" class="ltx_markedasmath" alttext="275\text{\,}\mathrm{mm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>275</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">mm</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">275\text{\,}\mathrm{mm}</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-C Visualization ‣ V RESULTS ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>). Scenario-wise,
S1 (static human) rarely required replanning; S2 (walk-by) produced
single, short replans that skirted the predicted corridor; and S3
(occlusion) produced earlier, conservative triggers but still completed
the task without contact. Replanning latency ranged from
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.0</annotation></semantics></math> s, so holds were brief and did not disrupt task tempo.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Sensitivity to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> is observed as expected:
lower <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> increases the frequency of early, conservative detours,
whereas higher <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> reduces interventions at the expense of smaller
predicted margins. The reported results use a single setting (<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}20</annotation></semantics></math>)
that balanced intervention rate and throughput across S1–S3.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Visualization</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qualitative behavior:</span> Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ V-A Planning Performance ‣ V RESULTS ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> illustrates the nominal
case in which the forecaster does not predict any human intrusion along
the planned trajectory. The APF score <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> for all sampled future
waypoints stays below the threshold <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, so the UR16e follows the
original (yellow) path end-to-end without invoking the planner. The
three panels show the start, an intermediate waypoint set, and the goal
pose, highlighting that execution is uninterrupted when predicted
proximities remain safe.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proactive avoidance:</span> Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V-A Planning Performance ‣ V RESULTS ‣ Digital-Twin Evaluation for Proactive Human–Robot Collision Avoidance via Prediction-Guided A-RRT∗" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows a time-ordered
sequence during proactive avoidance. The predictor first anticipates the
operator’s arm entering the shared workspace, which raises the APF for
upcoming waypoints above <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Execution pauses and the A-RRT* trees
expand while APF-unsafe nodes are pruned; a feasible corridor around the
predicted human region is found and smoothed. The new path is committed
and executed, maintaining <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;\,$250\text{\,}\mathrm{mm}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.448em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>250</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">mm</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;\,$250\text{\,}\mathrm{mm}$</annotation></semantics></math> clearance with only
a minimal increase in path length and execution time.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE I</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Planning time per (re)plan as reported in respective sources.</span></figcaption>
